    "chapters": [
      {
        "body": "The morning lay still over the harbour. Between the masts the archive shimmered like a memory made of glass.\n\nMara stopped at the end of the jetty. Today she would learn why the maps had been changing their lines for three nights.\n\nUnder her coat she carried the last sheet from her father's estate. The coastline on it did not end at the sea. It ran on, as if someone had drawn a path through the water.\n\nAt the gate of the Tidal Archive a man detached himself from the shadows. He held no lamp, although the windows behind him were dark.\n\n“You're late,” said Iven Rook.\n\nMara looked at the salt stains on his boots. “You were waiting for someone else.”\n\nHe did not answer. Over the harbour the first bell struck, and on the sheet in her pocket a line shifted.",
        "id": "c-arrival",
        "mentions": [
          {
            "confidence": 1,
            "elementId": "iven",
            "from": 561,
            "id": "m-iven",
            "source": "deterministic",
            "surface": "Iven Rook",
            "to": 570
          }
        ],
        "note": "Only hint at the city's unrest. Establish the archive as a quiet counterweight.",
        "storyTime": {
          "startMomentId": "t1"
        },
        "title": "The Arrival"
      },
      {
        "body": "In the reading room the keeper of the maps was already waiting. On the long table lay an atlas whose pages were thinner than ice.\n\nIven laid Mara's sheet next to the first map. Both coastlines began to glow. Outside, clouds drifted over the harbour, but here the light fell from below, through the glass.\n\nAt the edge of the line lay a glimmerline, so fine that one took it for a reflection. “He never showed this,” said Iven quietly. Then he closed the book as if he had already said too much.",
        "id": "c-archive",
        "marks": [
          {
            "from": 336,
            "kind": "italic",
            "to": 347
          }
        ],
        "note": "First encounter with the Cartographers' Guild.",
        "storyTime": {
          "startMomentId": "t2"
        },
        "title": "The Archive"
      },
      {
        "body": "Mara spread the sheet on the table and smoothed it with the back of her hand. In one place the ink had run, as if it had still been wet when her father put the pen aside.\n\nShe read the coastline like a letter: first the beginning, then the places where the hand had trembled. The atlas answered every stroke with a shimmer.\n\n***\n\nLate in the evening Iven counted the lamps in the reading room. There were fewer than in the morning, and neither of them asked who had taken them.",
        "id": "c-sheet",
        "note": "Let the sheet be read as a letter from the father.",
        "storyTime": {
          "startMomentId": "t3"
        },
        "title": "The Last Sheet"
      },
      {
        "body": "At the Salt Market nobody spoke loudly about the maps, and that was exactly why Mara heard about them everywhere. A trader claimed the coast had moved a boat's length overnight. A boy sold chalk marks that supposedly showed the way through the fog.\n\nOda Brandt stood at the edge of the square, counting barrels without looking at them. “Whoever asks about the archive will soon be asking me about bridges,” she said. “And tomorrow they're closed.”\n\nMara bought one of the chalk marks. It drew a line that led into the water.",
        "id": "c-market",
        "note": "Scatter rumours. Introduce Oda Brandt as an opponent without hostility.",
        "storyTime": {
          "startMomentId": "t4"
        },
        "title": "Rumours at the Salt Market"
      },
      {
        "body": "The Harbour Council had ordered the bridges closed. Only at the Tidal Archive was a light still burning. Mara now knew that the Guild was not searching for a lost route. It wanted to make sure no one found it.",
        "id": "c-storm",
        "note": "Sharpen the conflict between the Guild and the Harbour Council.",
        "storyTime": {
          "startMomentId": "t5"
        },
        "title": "Before the Storm"
      },
      {
        "body": "The Guild came at daybreak, in dark coats and with sealed folders. Their spokesman read out Iven Rook's name as if it were a verdict.\n\nIven stood between the shelves and did not move. Mara saw his hand go to his key ring and fall again.\n\n***\n\nLater she found him on a step in the archive courtyard. “I drew for them,” he said. “For twenty years. I just never knew what for.” Mara sat down beside him and said nothing.",
        "id": "c-break",
        "note": "Show Iven switching sides, but do not explain it.",
        "storyTime": {
          "startMomentId": "t6"
        },
        "title": "Break with the Guild"
      },
      {
        "body": "At the Ferry Jetty lay a single boat without a name, a lantern at its bow. Tilo Krey checked the lines as if he had been expecting them.\n\n“In this current I'll take you as far as the cliff,” he said. “No ferry goes further.” Lumen settled on the mast and looked ahead.\n\nIn the fog Tilo let go of the tiller and put it into Mara's hands. He climbed into the dinghy, raised the lantern and called after her to follow the line. The light drew away and did not come back.",
        "id": "c-crossing",
        "note": "Keep Tilo's farewell quiet. No pathos, only the lantern.",
        "title": "The Crossing"
      },
      {
        "body": "At daybreak they cast off. Behind them the Lighthouse Cliff sank into the fog. Ahead of them the atlas showed an open coast for the first time.\n\nMara laid the last sheet on the open page. The two lines ran into each other, and above the water a path of light appeared.",
        "id": "c-coast",
        "note": "End openly: the path is there, what lies at its end stays unsaid.",
        "title": "The Open Coast"
      }
    ],
